        "title": "The Strategy Pattern",
        "excerpt":" The Strategy Pattern defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. Strategy lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it.              Encapsulated behavior with the strategy pattern.   The Duck class that declares two reference variables for the behavior interface types. All duck subclasses inherit these.   public abstract class Duck { \tFlyBehavior flyBehavior; \tQuackBehavior quackBehavior;  \tpublic Duck() { \t}  \tpublic void setFlyBehavior(FlyBehavior fb) { \t\tflyBehavior = fb; \t}  \tpublic void setQuackBehavior(QuackBehavior qb) { \t\tquackBehavior = qb; \t}  \tabstract void display();  \tpublic void performFly() { \t\tflyBehavior.fly(); \t}  \tpublic void performQuack() { \t\tquackBehavior.quack(); \t}  \tpublic void swim() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"All ducks float, even decoys!\"); \t} }  The interface that all flying behavior classes implement.   public interface FlyBehavior { \tpublic void fly(); }  And some implementations of this interface:   public class FlyWithWings implements FlyBehavior { \tpublic void fly() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"I'm flying!!\"); \t} }  public class FlyNoWay implements FlyBehavior { \tpublic void fly() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"I can't fly\"); \t} }  public class FlyRocketPowered implements FlyBehavior { \tpublic void fly() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"I'm flying with a rocket\"); \t} }  The quack behavior interface is similar to the fly behavior interface.   public interface QuackBehavior { \tpublic void quack(); }  The following code snippets show two implementations of that interface:   public class Quack implements QuackBehavior { \tpublic void quack() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"Quack\"); \t} }  public class MuteQuack implements QuackBehavior { \tpublic void quack() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"&lt;&lt; Silence &gt;&gt;\"); \t} }  A subclass of Duck inherits the quackBehavior and flyBehavior instance variables. If performQuack() is called on the quackBehavior member the responsibility for the quack is delegated to the Quack behavior in the following example.   public class MallardDuck extends Duck {  \tpublic MallardDuck() { \t\tquackBehavior = new Quack(); \t\tflyBehavior = new FlyWithWings(); \t}  \tpublic void display() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"I'm a real Mallard duck\"); \t} }  The following main application shows that with the strategy pattern it is possible to change the behavior at runtime.   public class MiniDuckSimulator {  \tpublic static void main(String[] args) {  \t\tDuck mallard = new MallardDuck(); \t\tmallard.performQuack(); \t\tmallard.performFly();  \t\tDuck model = new ModelDuck(); \t\tmodel.performFly(); \t\tmodel.setFlyBehavior(new FlyRocketPowered()); \t\tmodel.performFly();  \t} }  Starting this application results in the following output.   $java MiniDuckSimulator Quack I’m flying!! I can’t fly I’m flying with a rocket  ","categories": ["programming","design patterns"],
        "tags": ["programming","design patterns","strategy","pattern","behavioral"],
        "url": "/design-patterns/strategy",
