        "title": "The Observer Pattern",
        "excerpt":" The Observer Pattern defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes state, all of its dependents are notified and updated automatically.    The observer pattern consists of publishers and subscribers similar to a newspaper subscription, where the publisher is also known as subject or observable and the subscribers are called observers.   The observer pattern provides an object design where subjects and observers are loosely coupled because of the following reasons:     The only thing the subject knows about an observer is that it implements a certain interface (the Observer interface).   We can add new observers at any time.   We never need to modify the subject to add new types of observers.   We can reuse subjects or observers independently of each other.   Changes to either the subject or an observer will not affect the other.   With the observer pattern it is possible to pull or push data from the observable whereas pull is considered more correct.   It is also important to note that you should not depend on a specific order of notification for your observers.             Loosely coupled objects with the observer pattern.   Here is the interface of a subject that a concrete subject class needs to implement in order to register, remove and notify observers.   public interface Subject { \tpublic void registerObserver(Observer o); \tpublic void removeObserver(Observer o); \tpublic void notifyObservers(); }  As an example for a concrete subject the following weather data class is given. It has a member ArrayList&lt;Observer&gt; that stores the currently registered observers.   import java.util.*;  public class WeatherData implements Subject { \tprivate ArrayList&lt;Observer&gt; observers; \tprivate float temperature; \tprivate float humidity; \tprivate float pressure;  \tpublic WeatherData() { \t\tobservers = new ArrayList&lt;Observer&gt;(); \t}  \tpublic void registerObserver(Observer o) { \t\tobservers.add(o); \t}  \tpublic void removeObserver(Observer o) { \t\tint i = observers.indexOf(o); \t\tif (i &gt;= 0) { \t\t\tobservers.remove(i); \t\t} \t}  \tpublic void notifyObservers() { \t\tfor (Observer observer : observers) { \t\t\tobserver.update(temperature, humidity, pressure); \t\t} \t}  \tpublic void measurementsChanged() { \t\tnotifyObservers(); \t}  \tpublic void setMeasurements(float temperature, float humidity, float pressure) { \t\tthis.temperature = temperature; \t\tthis.humidity = humidity; \t\tthis.pressure = pressure; \t\tmeasurementsChanged(); \t}  \tpublic float getTemperature() { \t\treturn temperature; \t}  \tpublic float getHumidity() { \t\treturn humidity; \t}  \tpublic float getPressure() { \t\treturn pressure; \t}  }  When the notifyObservers() function gets called, which happens when some data of the subject changes, the subject knows how to inform the observers because they all implement the same interface.   public interface Observer { \tpublic void update(float temp, float humidity, float pressure); }  The observers that implement this interface know about the subject, which allows them to register themselves to be kept informed. This can be seen in the following example that also implements another interface DisplayElement.   public class CurrentConditionsDisplay implements Observer, DisplayElement { \tprivate float temperature; \tprivate float humidity; \tprivate Subject weatherData;  \tpublic CurrentConditionsDisplay(Subject weatherData) { \t\tthis.weatherData = weatherData; \t\tweatherData.registerObserver(this); \t}  \tpublic void update(float temperature, float humidity, float pressure) { \t\tthis.temperature = temperature; \t\tthis.humidity = humidity; \t\tdisplay(); \t}  \tpublic void display() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"Current conditions: \" + temperature \t\t\t+ \"F degrees and \" + humidity + \"% humidity\"); \t} }  Another example of a observer implementation.   public class StatisticsDisplay implements Observer, DisplayElement { \tprivate float maxTemp = 0.0f; \tprivate float minTemp = 200; \tprivate float tempSum= 0.0f; \tprivate int numReadings; \tprivate WeatherData weatherData;  \tpublic StatisticsDisplay(WeatherData weatherData) { \t\tthis.weatherData = weatherData; \t\tweatherData.registerObserver(this); \t}  \tpublic void update(float temp, float humidity, float pressure) { \t\ttempSum += temp; \t\tnumReadings++;  \t\tif (temp &gt; maxTemp) { \t\t\tmaxTemp = temp; \t\t}  \t\tif (temp &lt; minTemp) { \t\t\tminTemp = temp; \t\t}  \t\tdisplay(); \t}  \tpublic void display() { \t\tSystem.out.println(\"Avg/Max/Min temperature = \" + (tempSum / numReadings) \t\t\t+ \"/\" + maxTemp + \"/\" + minTemp); \t} }  And the additional interface they use.   public interface DisplayElement { \tpublic void display(); }  The following main WeatherStation class shows the observer pattern in use.   public class WeatherStation {  \tpublic static void main(String[] args) { \t\tWeatherData weatherData = new WeatherData();  \t\tCurrentConditionsDisplay currentDisplay = \t\t\tnew CurrentConditionsDisplay(weatherData); \t\tStatisticsDisplay statisticsDisplay = new StatisticsDisplay(weatherData);  \t\tweatherData.setMeasurements(80, 65, 30.4f); \t\tweatherData.setMeasurements(82, 70, 29.2f); \t} }  Starting this application results in the following output.   $java WeatherStation Current conditions: 80.0F degrees and 65.0% humidity Avg/Max/Min temperature = 80.0/80.0/80.0 Current conditions: 82.0F degrees and 70.0% humidity Avg/Max/Min temperature = 81.0/82.0/80.0  ","categories": ["programming","design patterns"],
        "tags": ["programming","design patterns","observer","pattern","behavioral"],
        "url": "/design-patterns/observer",
        "teaser": "/assets/pages/design-patterns/observer-pattern.png"
